WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.943
The prospect asked for something to share with the team.

2
00:00:03.993 --> 00:00:05.417
You sent a PDF.

3
00:00:05.467 --> 00:00:09.192
Here's why a narrated pitch deck makes the case when you're not there.

4
00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:12.199
Sales slides are built to be presented.

5
00:00:12.249 --> 00:00:19.155
Without your voice, readers jump to pricing, your champion retells it from memory, and every slide gets the same weight.

6
00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:23.063
Keep it short and specific to the buyer.

7
00:00:23.113 --> 00:00:28.369
Their situation, the problem, your answer, proof, then one clear ask.

8
00:00:28.419 --> 00:00:30.461
It mirrors how a buyer thinks.

9
00:00:30.961 --> 00:00:34.255
Headlines should tell the story with the sound off.

10
00:00:34.305 --> 00:00:38.388
Your speaker notes become the narration, so write them the way you'd say them.

11
00:00:38.438 --> 00:00:40.747
If you trip, the narrator will too.

12
00:00:41.997 --> 00:00:47.184
Many people watch forwarded videos with the sound off, so turn on burned-in captions.

13
00:00:47.234 --> 00:00:50.680
Then send a brief email that states the length and the ask.

14
00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:55.119
Ordinary slides in, extraordinary pitch out.

15
00:00:55.169 --> 00:01:01.864
